\list defines a list of items.
Its syntax is the following:

\list name {c' e' g' bes'}

The name is given without the backslash, and once the list
is defined it is called with a backslash. A list can also
be written in more lines:

\list name {
    c' e'
    g' bes'
}

An item is retrieved with its index (1-based), after a dot.
When used as an argument to another command, it is replaced
by the item, so the line below prints e':

\print \name.2

The traverse method executes the lines inside its curly
brackets once for every item of the list, replacing $ with
the item:

\name.traverse {
    \myosc.send "/note" $
}

Errors worth knowing: indexes start at 1, an index greater
than the number of items is out of range, a list name
can't be redefined, and it can't be the name of a LiveLily
command.
